Now the fix. Design: add a `verbatim` list for split tool groups. When the inner loop breaks with `answered.isNotEmpty`, the group crossed a non-matching message (stering interleave): some result answers from the kept tail (or from beyond the cut). Keep the call — and the results already matched — verbatim; do NOT consume the matched results into carriers. Then those verbatim messages go into the payload unmerged, before the carriers (position within the droppable region doesn't matter semantically; repairToolPairing hoists results next to their calls).

Implementation:

```dart
   if (calls.isEmpty) {
     units.add([message]);
     i++;
     continue;
   }
   final answered = calls.map((call) => call.id).toSet();
   final group = <Message>[message];
   var j = i + 1;
   while (j < messages.length && answered.isNotEmpty) {
     final next = messages[j];
     if (next is ToolResultMessage && answered.remove(next.toolCallId)) {
       group.add(next);
       j++;
     } else {
       break;
     }
   }
   if (answered.isNotEmpty) {
     // PR #1695: the group hit a non-matching message before every call
     // was answered — a steering user message interleaved between the
     // call and its result. Merging the call into a carrier would orphan
     // the result (repair drops it) and lose the tool content; keep the
     // call and the results already paired with it VERBATIM instead — the
     // pairing repair hoists the displaced result back next to the call.
     // The still-unmatched results are NOT consumed: they stay in the
     // stream for their own units/tail.
     verbatim.addAll(group);
     i++;
     continue;
   }
   units.add(group);
   i = j;
```

Wait: careful — after verbatim handling with `i++`, the matched results in `group` were at indexes i+1..j-1. With i++ we move to i+1 which is the first matched result → it'll be processed as its own unit → merged into a carrier — but we're keeping it verbatim in `verbatim`! Double-processing: the result would appear BOTH verbatim and in a carrier. Bad.

